@design/task-core.sigil from TaskCore import {
  task id, brief, child run, failure kind, domain event, child port, session usage,
    launch spec, attach command, child session, session export, child driver,
    child working directory,
    starter tools, prerequisites check,
}
@design/agent-config.sigil from AgentConfig import {
  agent definition, skill folder, project root, timeout, thinking level,
  tool list, general agent, fallback agent,
}
@design/task-store.sigil from TaskStore import {
  task record, sessions folder, driver files,
}
@design/kernel.sigil from Kernel import { driver registry, entry point, child host }

component PiDriver {
  goal {
    Start an isolated pi child that sees only the declared skills, run one
    brief to
    settlement, and stop it cleanly in every exit case.
  }

  state {
    PiDriver fills the launch spec of each child run with the argv of pi
    without its prompt, its child working directory, the names of its environment
    variables, the pi version, its skill folder paths and the thinking level of
    the agent definition.

    A *skill overlay* is the folder skills/ inside the folder of the
    driver files
    that the child port hands over for the task. It is owned by PiDriver and is
    not
    part of the task record, and it holds a copy of each declared skill folder
    side
    by side, so that relative paths between sibling skills keep working. The
    skill overlay is among the driver files of the task.

    The child session of a task is the pi conversation of that task, kept as a
    pi
    session file.
  }

  logic {
    Before spawning, check that every skill folder of the agent definition
    exists,
    and stop with the failure kind skill_mismatch if one is missing. Copy each
    declared skill folder into the skill overlay, whose paths the launch spec
    uses.
    Hand the launch spec to the caller in the run started domain event before
    spawning, which ends that branch. Spawn pi from the launch spec as the
    leader
    of its own process group, with the project root as child working directory as
    the
    constraints require. Send get_state, whose reply means pi is ready, and
    then
    get_commands, whose reply goes to skill verification. Perform
    *skill verification* by comparing the skills in the get_commands reply with
    the
    declared skills. On a difference, stop with the failure kind skill_mismatch
    naming the extra or missing skill, send no prompt and kill the process
    group.

    Otherwise, once skill verification has passed as the constraints require,
    send
    the brief as the prompt and read events until agent_settled. Map each
    *pi event* to a domain event and hand it to the caller, which ends that
    branch.
    After agent_settled, read the last assistant text and the session usage,
    close
    stdin and wait for the child run to exit, and the exit lets the flow
    return.
    Return the last assistant text, the session usage, a status taken from the
    stop
    reason of the last assistant message and, for a failed run, its
    failure kind,
    which ends the flow. A stop reason of stop means completed, length means
    truncated, error means failed with the failure kind error and a short
    bounded
    message from pi, and aborted means cancelled with no failure kind. A
    child run
    that exits cleanly with no assistant message is failed with the
    failure kind
    error and the message no assistant message. Fill the session usage from
    what pi
    reports, and mark each field that pi does not report as unavailable.
  }

  constraints {
    The driver must start a child run with exactly this flag set in its
    launch spec, with the project root as child working directory and with
    PI_OFFLINE=1
    and PI_TELEMETRY=0 in its environment. It must not add --session, because
    pi
    exits when that is combined with --session-id:
    ```
    pi --mode rpc --offline -na -ne -nc -np --no-themes -ns
       --skill <overlay>/<name> ... --model <provider/id> --thinking <level>
       --tools <tools> --session-dir <sessions folder>
       --session-id <task id> --name subzero:<agent>:<task id>
    ```

    PiDriver receives the project root, the sessions folder and the folder of
    its
    driver files through the child port each time it starts a child run,
    resolves
    none of its own, and calls neither TaskStore nor the delegation service.
    PiDriver must not write the task record, and hands the
    launch spec to the caller so that TaskCore writes it.

    A child run must see exactly the declared skills and no user or project
    extensions, context files, prompt templates or other skills. It must run
    under
    the user's own pi login, and no credentials may be copied.

    A missing skill folder, or any difference found by skill verification, must
    fail the call as skill_mismatch before the brief is sent.

    The driver must use its own *pi RPC client* for pi 1.5.2, the version the
    plan
    targets, and must verify its child flags on that version before use, and
    not
    the
    client that
    pi exports. It must split stdout on LF only, strip a trailing CR, decode
    UTF-8
    from a byte buffer, correlate each response to its command by id, and never
    use
    Node readline. A reply to a command of a child run must not be subject to
    the
    60-second wait of the exported client.

    The status of a child run that the driver ends by timeout, cancel, crash or
    unknown model must come from that termination and not from the stop reason
    of
    its last assistant message, and so must its failure kind when it is failed.
    When a cancel and a timeout coincide, the first termination wins and is
    recorded once, and a cancel that arrives after the child run has settled
    changes nothing.

    A child run settles only at agent_settled, never at agent_end.

    A pi event of an unrecognized type must be logged and ignored, and the
    child run must continue.

    Agent message deltas must be buffered into one domain event and not
    forwarded
    per token.

    The timeout of the agent definition must start when the driver spawns the
    child run and cover skill verification and the turn, and it must not count
    the time that the child run waited Pending for its caller.

    When a child run exceeds its timeout, exits non-zero in mid-turn or names
    an
    unknown model, it must end with the failure kind with value timeout, crashed or
    error.
    A
    crashed child run records the stderr tail as a domain event, and an unknown
    model error names the model. A crashed child run is a process failure,
    which is
    a non-zero exit in mid-turn or a lost pipe, and an error is a run-level
    error
    that pi reports through its stop reason, or an unknown model.

    The driver must not remove the skill overlay of a task while its
    task record
    exists, because pi ignores a missing --skill path without an error and an
    attached child session would then have no skills.

    Cancel and timeout of a child run must send clear_queue, then abort and
    wait up
    to five seconds, then close stdin, then send SIGTERM and then SIGKILL to
    the
    process group. No process of the group may remain, including a grandchild.

    The driver must not unref a child run or let it outlive its process: the
    child run starts as the leader of its own process group only so that the
    whole
    group
    can be killed, and it keeps the pipes of its stdio. When the process that
    runs
    the driver dies, stdin of the child run is closed and pi must exit, so no
    process of a child run may outlive Esc, a timeout or the end of the parent
    session.

    When ~/.pi/agent/SYSTEM.md exists, the driver must record a warning as a
    domain event and still start the child run, because -nc does not cover it.

    The id of the child session must be the task id, and the sessions folder
    must
    be inside the project root and not the user's own pi sessions.
  }

  cases {
    When skill verification finds exactly the declared skills, the brief is
    sent
    and the child run settles with the last assistant text as its summary.

    When skill verification finds one extra skill, the child run fails as
    skill_mismatch naming it, no prompt is sent and the process is killed.

    When pi emits agent_end, then more events, then agent_settled, the
    child run
    settles only at agent_settled.

    A message of a child run that contains U+2028 is delivered as one record,
    and a
    multi-byte UTF-8 character split across two stdout chunks decodes
    correctly.
  }

  interface {
    PiDriver provides the child port: for an agent definition, a brief and a
    task id, start a child run, perform skill verification, run the brief to
    settlement, and return the last assistant text, the status, the
    failure kind of
    a failed run and the session usage. Allow a live child run to be cancelled.
    Keep the skill overlay of each task for as long as its task record exists.

    Hand each domain event of a live child run to the caller as it happens,
    with
    the tool name and its target path in the domain event of each tool
    activity.

    Build the attach command from the launch spec of a task, without --mode rpc
    and without --session, so that the child session can be reopened in the pi
    terminal interface.

    Hand the launch spec of every child run to the caller in the run started
    domain event, so that the task record keeps it.

    Export the child session of a task as HTML with pi --export, as the
    session export of the child driver for the child host pi.

    Offer a prerequisites check that runs the configured pi command with
    --version, passes when it exits zero, and otherwise returns a message that
    names pi, says whether it was not found or did not run, and says to install
    pi and run subzero init again.

    Register with the driver registry of Kernel as the child driver for the
    child host pi
    when an entry point of a process registers it, and offer the
    attach command,
    the session export, the starter tools and the prerequisites check through
    it.
    The starter tools are the tool list read, bash, edit, write, grep, find and
    ls for a general agent, and the tool list read, grep, find and ls for a
    fallback agent.
  }

  decisions {
    The driver spawns the user's installed pi and does not embed the pi SDK, so
    that it uses the same login, models and version, can kill by process group,
    and
    a crash in the child cannot take down the server. A failure kind is still
    decided by the driver from what pi did.
  }
}
